    faqs: [
      { question: "What is the difference between APR and interest rate?", answer: "The interest rate is the cost of borrowing the principal expressed as a yearly percentage and does not include lender fees. APR adds those fees — origination charges, points, closing costs and certain other charges — to give the true all-in annual cost. Regulations require lenders to disclose APR so borrowers can make apples-to-apples comparisons. On a mortgage, APR is often 0.1–0.5 percentage points higher than the rate; on personal loans with high origination fees, the gap can be much larger." },
      { question: "Why is APR always higher than the stated interest rate?", answer: "Because APR incorporates upfront fees that are effectively prepaid interest. When you pay an origination fee, you receive less net cash but repay the full loan amount — so the effective cost per dollar borrowed is higher than the stated rate suggests. The only exception is if a lender offers loan discounts or credits that offset fees, which can theoretically make the APR equal to or lower than the rate. In practice, APR equals the rate only on no-fee loans." },
      { question: "What is a good APR for a personal loan?", answer: "Personal loan APRs range from around 6% for well-qualified borrowers to over 36% for subprime borrowers. As a rough benchmark: under 10% is excellent, 10–15% is good, 15–20% is average for mid-tier credit, and above 20% is expensive. Credit cards average around 20–24% APR. The best APR you can qualify for depends on your credit score, income, and debt-to-income ratio. Comparing at least three lenders via prequalification — which uses a soft credit pull — is the most reliable way to find your true market rate." },
      { question: "How do I compare loans using APR?", answer: "Collect APR figures from multiple lenders for the same loan amount and term, making sure each lender's disclosed APR includes the same categories of fees. A lower APR means lower total cost of borrowing, all else equal. However, also consider prepayment penalties, which can trap you in a high-rate loan, and whether the APR assumes a variable rate that could rise. For very short-term loans, even small fee differences matter more because the amortization period is brief and fees consume a larger share of total payments." },
      { question: "Does APR include all loan costs?", answer: "Not necessarily. US law requires APR to include interest, origination fees, points and certain other charges, but it typically excludes optional fees (credit insurance), third-party costs paid to non-lender parties (title insurance, appraisals), and late fees or prepayment penalties. This is why mortgages have multiple APR figures — the regulatory APR and sometimes an all-in APR — and why reading the full loan disclosure carefully is essential even after comparing APRs across lenders." },
    ],
