    formula: "Surplus = income − total expenses; Savings rate = savings / income × 100",
    explanation: "A budget is the foundational tool of personal finance — a plan that assigns your income to specific categories before the month begins, ensuring your spending aligns with your priorities. This budget calculator uses the 50/30/20 rule popularized by US Senator Elizabeth Warren in her book All Your Worth, which provides a simple framework for allocating after-tax income. Fifty percent is allocated to needs: housing, utilities, food, essential transportation, insurance, and minimum debt payments — the non-negotiable expenses. Thirty percent goes to wants: dining out, entertainment, subscriptions, travel, and hobbies. Twenty percent is reserved for savings and debt repayment beyond the minimums. This budget calculator reveals your current allocation and shows whether your surplus or deficit requires adjustment. A surplus signals room to increase savings or accelerate debt payoff. A deficit means spending must be reduced or income increased — and the category breakdown reveals where the most impactful cuts can be made. Budgeting is a skill that improves with practice; even imprecise budgets beat no budget at all. Tracking spending for just three months gives most people a clear picture of their patterns and a realistic baseline for reducing discretionary costs.",
    formulaExplanation: "Total expenses are summed across all input categories. Surplus is income minus total expenses. Savings rate is savings divided by income, expressed as a percentage. The 50/30/20 targets are income × 0.50 for needs, income × 0.30 for wants, and income × 0.20 for savings/debt. The housing percentage divides housing cost by total income and reveals whether housing is consuming a disproportionate share of budget.",
