    faqs: [
      { question: "What is the 50/30/20 budgeting rule?", answer: "The 50/30/20 rule divides after-tax income into three buckets: 50% for needs (essential expenses you cannot avoid — housing, food, utilities, insurance, minimum debt payments), 30% for wants (discretionary spending that enhances life — dining out, entertainment, subscriptions, travel), and 20% for savings and extra debt repayment. It provides a simple starting framework rather than a detailed line-item budget. Many people find it easier to maintain than zero-based budgeting because it allows flexibility within each bucket without tracking every transaction." },
      { question: "What is the difference between needs and wants in a budget?", answer: "Needs are expenses that are necessary to live and meet your obligations: shelter, basic food, utilities, essential transportation, health insurance, and minimum debt payments you are legally required to make. Wants are expenses that improve your quality of life but could be reduced or eliminated without threatening your basic welfare: dining out versus cooking at home, a streaming service, new clothes beyond what you need, gym memberships, and vacations. The distinction is sometimes fuzzy — a car is a need in most US cities but a luxury vehicle is a want." },
      { question: "What is a healthy savings rate?", answer: "Financial advisors commonly recommend saving at least 20% of gross income, which aligns with the 20% portion of the 50/30/20 rule applied to take-home pay. If you contribute 10% to a 401k and save another 10% for other goals, you are in solid shape. The higher your savings rate, the faster you build wealth — at a 50% savings rate, you can retire in roughly 17 years from a standing start under typical market assumptions. Even a 10–15% rate puts you on track for a comfortable retirement if started early." },
      { question: "How do I stick to a budget?", answer: "The most effective budgeting strategy is the one you will actually maintain. Popular methods include the envelope system (physically or digitally allocating cash to categories), zero-based budgeting (assigning every dollar a purpose each month), and the 50/30/20 automatic approach (automating savings and bills, then spending freely within the remaining balance). Tracking apps like YNAB, Mint, or Personal Capital make it easier by linking to accounts and categorizing transactions automatically. The key behavioral insight is that awareness alone reduces spending — people who review spending weekly consistently spend less than those who do not." },
      { question: "How much should I budget for housing?", answer: "The 50/30/20 rule places housing within the 50% needs bucket, and a common sub-guideline is to keep housing costs under 30% of gross income. In high-cost cities like New York or San Francisco, this is often impossible, and some financial advisors accept 35–40% for renters in very expensive markets if other categories are tightly controlled. Homeownership adds property taxes, insurance, and maintenance (budget 1–2% of home value annually), so total housing costs can run well above the mortgage payment alone and must be factored into affordability calculations." },
    ],
