    formula: "Hourly = annual / (weeks × hours/week); Monthly = annual / 12; Biweekly = annual / 26",
    explanation: "Salary conversion translates your annual compensation into equivalent rates at different pay periods, which is essential for comparing job offers, negotiating raises, evaluating contract work, and building a realistic budget. This salary calculator performs four core conversions: annual to hourly (dividing by total annual work hours), monthly gross (dividing by 12), biweekly gross (dividing by 26 pay periods), and weekly. The hourly rate is particularly useful for salaried workers who want to evaluate their time against contractor rates or freelance opportunities. A standard full-time year in the US is 2,080 hours (52 weeks × 40 hours), which is the default denominator. If your schedule differs — a 37.5-hour week common in some white-collar roles gives 1,950 hours — adjust accordingly to get the accurate effective rate. When comparing a salaried offer to a contract rate, remember that employees receive additional benefits value beyond salary: employer health insurance contributions (often $6,000–$15,000 annually), employer 401k match, paid time off, and payroll taxes the employer covers. A contractor must earn substantially more per hour to net the equivalent total compensation. Running each figure through this salary calculator puts every offer on a common annual basis so comparisons are truly apples to apples.",
    formulaExplanation: "Total annual work hours = weeks per year × hours per week (default 52 × 40 = 2,080). Hourly = annual salary ÷ total hours. Monthly = annual ÷ 12. Biweekly = annual ÷ 26 (26 pay periods of two weeks each). Weekly = annual ÷ weeks per year. All conversions assume consistent year-round employment at the stated hours without adjusting for holidays, vacation, or sick days.",
