    formula: "Annual Income = Shares × Dividend Per Share; Yield = (DPS / Share Price) × 100; Projected = DPS × (1 + g)^n",
    formulaExplanation: "Annual income is simply shares owned multiplied by the annual dividend per share (the sum of all quarterly or monthly payments per year). Dividend yield expresses the annual dividend as a percentage of the current share price. For growth projection, future dividend per share after n years equals today's DPS times (1 + g) raised to the power n, where g is the annual growth rate expressed as a decimal. Total dividends sums each year's income over the projection period.",
    formulaSource: "Standard dividend investing analysis",
    explanation: "Dividend investing is one of the oldest and most tested income strategies in equity markets, offering regular cash payouts from profitable companies while retaining exposure to potential share price appreciation. This dividend calculator quantifies the income side of a dividend position and projects how that income grows over time for dividend growth investors. The current yield — annual dividend divided by share price — tells you the income rate if you bought today. But for long-term holders, yield on cost (dividend relative to what you originally paid) can be significantly higher as companies raise dividends over decades. Companies like Johnson & Johnson, Procter & Gamble, and Coca-Cola have increased their dividends for 50+ consecutive years. The dividend calculator's growth-rate input models this compounding: a 5% annual raise doubles the dividend payment in about 14 years. For retirement income planning, a $500,000 portfolio yielding 3.5% produces $17,500 in annual income that grows alongside dividend hikes, creating a natural inflation hedge without requiring asset sales. When evaluating dividend stocks, also check the payout ratio (dividends as a percentage of earnings) — ratios above 80–90% suggest the dividend may be unsustainable during an earnings downturn. A well-diversified dividend portfolio blends high-yield names with lower-yield, faster-growing dividend growers.",
