    formula: "Value after n years = Purchase Price × (1 − depreciation rate)^n",
    formulaExplanation: "The declining balance method applies the depreciation rate to the remaining value each year, not the original price. This creates the familiar curve where depreciation is heaviest in early years (when the value is highest) and slows as the car ages. After n years, the retained value equals the purchase price multiplied by (1 minus the rate) raised to the power of the number of years.",
    formulaSource: "IRS Publication 463 / MACRS depreciation methodology",
    explanation: "Vehicle depreciation is the single largest cost of car ownership that most people underestimate. A new car loses approximately 15–20% of its value in the first year alone, with another 10–15% in each subsequent year. By year five, most vehicles are worth only 35–50% of their original sticker price. Luxury vehicles and certain mainstream brands depreciate faster than average; trucks, SUVs, and some Japanese models hold their value better. This car depreciation calculator uses the declining-balance method, which applies a constant percentage to the vehicle's current value — matching real market behavior more closely than straight-line depreciation. A car depreciation calculator has practical applications beyond curiosity. If you buy a new car and sell in two or three years, the depreciation loss is a real cost embedded in your total transportation expense. Leasing, in theory, prices this depreciation into the monthly payment and returns the vehicle before the slow depreciation years, which is why monthly lease payments are lower than loan payments on the same vehicle. Keeping a car past the point of heavy depreciation (typically years six through ten) drops your cost per mile substantially — often the most economical approach to total ownership cost. Buying a vehicle two to three years old captures someone else's heavy depreciation loss and often still enjoys warranty coverage.",
