    formula: "Annual = Hourly Rate × Hours per Week × Weeks per Year",
    formulaExplanation: "Multiplying the hourly rate by weekly hours gives weekly earnings, and multiplying that by the number of annual work weeks gives the yearly total — capturing exactly how much time is sold at the stated price. Monthly pay is the annual figure divided by 12; biweekly is annual divided by 26 (26 two-week periods per year); weekly is hourly rate times hours per week. Daily pay assumes an 8-hour workday regardless of the weekly hours setting.",
    formulaSource: "Bureau of Labor Statistics — Wage Calculations",
    explanation: "An hourly wage calculator converts your pay to equivalent annual or periodic figures, essential for comparing job offers, evaluating a raise, negotiating salary, or building an accurate budget. An hourly worker's total annual earnings depend on three variables: the hourly rate, weekly hours, and how many weeks per year they work. Full-time employment is conventionally 40 hours per week for 52 weeks, yielding 2,080 annual hours — so $25/hour equals $52,000 per year. However, part-time workers, seasonal employees, independent contractors, and those with unpaid weeks off will have different annual totals. When comparing a salaried offer to an hourly position, consider that salaried workers may work more than 40 hours without additional compensation, effectively reducing their implied hourly rate. For example, a $60,000 salaried employee working 50 hours per week earns $23.08 per hour, while a $28/hour hourly employee working 40 hours earns $58,240 — close in total pay but with the hourly worker's extra hours compensated. In contrast, hourly workers may receive overtime pay (1.5× their regular rate) for hours worked beyond 40 per week under the Fair Labor Standards Act, which can significantly boost annual earnings for those who regularly work overtime. Use this hourly wage calculator to standardize all pay options into a common annual figure before making comparisons.",
