    formula: "Taxable Income = Gross − Pre-Tax Deductions − Standard Deduction; Federal Tax via 2024 brackets; FICA = 7.65% of gross (up to SS wage base)",
    formulaExplanation: "Pre-tax deductions (401k, health insurance, HSA/FSA) reduce taxable income dollar-for-dollar. The standard deduction ($14,600 single / $29,200 married for 2024) further reduces the taxable base. Federal income tax is computed using marginal brackets — the lowest portion is taxed at 10%, the next at 12%, and so on. FICA is Social Security tax (6.2%, capped at the $168,600 wage base for 2024) plus Medicare (1.45%, uncapped). State tax applies a flat effective rate to gross income as a simplification.",
    formulaSource: "IRS Publication 15 (2024 tax year)",
    explanation: "Your paycheck calculator reveals the significant gap between gross salary — the number quoted in a job offer — and net take-home pay, what actually reaches your bank account. For most full-time US employees, the gap is 25–40% of gross depending on income, location, and pre-tax elections. Federal income tax uses progressive marginal brackets: in 2024 the rates are 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35%, and 37%, applied successively to income above each threshold. Only income above each bracket threshold is taxed at that rate, not all income — a common misconception. Someone with $75,000 in taxable income does not pay 22% on all of it; they pay 10% on the first $11,600, 12% on the next $35,550, and 22% only on the rest. FICA taxes (Social Security 6.2% + Medicare 1.45%) are separate from income tax and apply to gross wages before deductions. Pre-tax benefit contributions to a 401k, health insurance premium, FSA, or HSA reduce both federal income tax and usually FICA. This makes pre-tax benefits doubly valuable: a $6,000 annual 401k contribution saves roughly $1,500 in federal income tax for someone in the 22% bracket plus about $459 in FICA — nearly $2,000 in annual savings. This paycheck calculator provides an estimate; actual liability may differ with additional income, itemized deductions, tax credits, and other factors.",
